    <h2>A first visit that is mostly fun</h2>
    <p>A child’s first visit sets the tone for years. So we keep it short and light: a ride up and down in the chair, a look at the “tooth camera”, a mirror to hold, and a count of teeth out loud. If they want to sit on your lap, they sit on your lap.</p>
    <h2>As they grow</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Toddlers:</strong> teething, brushing tips that work with a wriggling two-year-old, and fluoride varnish.</li>
        <li><strong>School age:</strong> check-ups, sealants on the new back teeth, and keeping an eye on how adult teeth are coming in.</li>
        <li><strong>Teenagers:</strong> mouthguards for sport, wisdom teeth questions, and honest conversations about whitening, aligners and energy drinks.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Words we never use</h2>
    <p>You will not hear “needle”, “drill” or “this won’t hurt” in Dr. Marchetti’s room. We use calm, honest words a child can understand, and we never promise something we cannot keep. Read more in our post on <a href="/blog/your-childs-first-dental-visit/">your child’s first dental visit</a>.</p>
